<InsightArticle title="Repeating a model trial multiplies calls before it improves the evidence" description="A frozen AIRH price record puts explicit costs beside repeated trial designs. What the extra calls establish, what they leave unknown and how to budget them honestly." date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="9 min" snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="At one frozen AIRH rate, 80 cases across four candidates cost $1.12 in a hypothetical one-pass trial. Three repetitions produce 960 calls and a $3.36 token estimate, before judging, tools or review.">
<p>Repeats are easy to add to a trial plan. Their price is multiplication; their value needs a reason. Running the same cases again can reveal changes between attempts, but the extra rows do not become new kinds of task. A trial report needs to preserve that distinction before presenting a large call count as broad coverage.</p>
<p>The dollar amounts here use the Mistral Large 4 row in AIRH's frozen 14 November 2026 snapshot, inspected for this guide on that date. The four candidates are hypothetical placeholders all priced at that same rate, not four measured models. The case count, token counts and repeats are teaching choices. No model requests or paid judging took place.</p>
<h2>One case can create several billable attempts</h2>
<p>A case is an input together with the written rule for deciding whether its answer is acceptable. An attempt is one execution of that case. A candidate is the complete setup being compared, including the model and prompt choices. These definitions keep the accounting clear when a case is executed several times under each setup.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={['Cases','Candidates','Repeats per case','Calls','Model-token dollars']} rows={d.trials.map(r=>[r.cases,r.candidates,r.repeats,r.calls,r.cost])} caption="Invented trial sizes, all candidate placeholders priced at one frozen rate: $0.70 per million input and $2.10 per million output tokens. Each call assumes 2,000 input and 1,000 output tokens. No model performance is measured." />
<p>Each assumed call costs $0.0035: 2,000 times the recorded input rate plus 1,000 times the recorded output rate, divided by a million. The first row contains 320 calls. Three repeats give 960 and five give 1,600. Their costs follow directly from the number of attempts, with no claim that those output counts would occur in practice.</p>
<p>Pricing every candidate identically isolates the cost of repetition. A real comparison would substitute each candidate's own applicable rates and charged usage. Otherwise a precise total would conceal differences in response lengths or billing categories. The teaching amounts should remain in the trial plan as examples, rather than being copied into an operational spending approval.</p>
<h2>The extra rows answer a narrower question</h2>
<p>Repeated attempts can show whether the same case passes consistently under a fixed setup. They do not, by themselves, show that the case set represents the incoming workload. A collection of repeated short questions still contains only those short questions. The description of coverage should therefore name the distinct cases and their categories separately from the total attempt count.</p>
<p>A category means a group of tasks that share a relevant feature, such as extracting a field or checking an arithmetic result. The grouping should reflect the intended use. This article prescribes no universal category list. Its proposal is simply that the report retain enough structure to show where the attempts came from, instead of compressing the trial into one impressive total.</p>
<p>A case that passes once and fails later deserves a visible record. Collapsing its attempts into a single pass can hide the inconsistency the repeats were meant to reveal. Different summaries answer different questions: whether it ever passed, whether it always passed in the observed attempts, or what fraction of its attempts passed. The chosen summary needs an explicit definition.</p>
<p>None of those observed summaries guarantees future behaviour. The table contains no statistical claim or uncertainty interval. Its financial arithmetic remains valid under the assumed counts even when the trial design is unsuitable for estimating a future success rate. A low token bill does not make a weak evaluation adequate.</p>
<h2>Changing the setup starts another comparison</h2>
<p>Suppose the prompt is repaired after inspecting a failure. The next attempt now tests a changed candidate. That can be useful development work, but merging it into the original run would obscure which configuration produced the result. A versioned record lets the team improve the prompt without pretending the entire attempt set came from one fixed setup.</p>
<p>The same applies to changing tools or the pass rule during a trial. A pass rule is the written test of what counts as acceptable. If that rule becomes easier, a higher later pass count may reflect the standard rather than better answers. Preserving the earlier rule and results lets a reviewer see the actual reason for the change.</p>
<p>For a paired comparison, each candidate receives the same defined cases under the intended conditions. Paired means that results can be matched case by case. This makes it possible to see where one candidate succeeds and another fails, rather than relying only on unrelated totals. Repeats can remain nested inside those case records.</p>
<p>Extra attempts on a case already used to tune the prompt are still observations on that development case. Calling them held out would misdescribe their role. A held-out set is one kept separate from the development choices it will evaluate. This guide does not supply such a set or claim that a particular split would be sufficient.</p>
<h2>The call plan needs its omissions beside it</h2>
<p>The table includes model-token charges only. It excludes automated judges, external tools, retries, taxes and human time. Each exclusion is a possible separate line in a real plan, not evidence that the charge is zero. An application that calls a judge after each response needs to account for that activity on the corresponding number of attempts.</p>
<p>Unsuccessful attempts also need an accounting outcome. A timeout might consume elapsed time while leaving a charge unresolved. A failed response should not silently disappear from the attempt ledger because it lacks a useful answer. The service's usage and billing records, where available, are the evidence for charges rather than an assumption that failure means free.</p>
<p>An enforced spending cap and an estimate serve different purposes. The estimate predicts a bill under assumptions; the cap is intended to prevent further authorised work after a boundary. This article implements neither provider calls nor spending controls. A team running a trial would need to establish how its own application enforces the limit before relying on it.</p>
<p>It may be reasonable to run fewer repetitions and more distinct cases, or the reverse, depending on the question. The article does not pick an optimum. It gives the decision a visible cost and asks the plan to explain what evidence the additional attempts are expected to provide. That explanation should be about the use case, not the convenience of multiplying a runner setting.</p>
<p>The <a href="/transparency/#practitioner-planning">source and formula receipt</a> ties the displayed amounts to a frozen catalogue row. It preserves that row's source label and import time without claiming a fresh vendor verification. Live prices, current availability and actual usage need separate evidence before an operational trial budget is approved.</p>
<p>Case order can be recorded as part of the run rather than left to recollection. If later attempts use different evidence or service conditions, that belongs with the results. This is a proposal for preserving interpretability, not a claim that one ordering method eliminates all sources of variation. Reproducibility starts with describing what actually happened clearly enough for another person to inspect it.</p>
<p>The four-candidate example also leaves the identity of the setups open on purpose. It prices the size of a proposed experiment, rather than making an undocumented claim about a particular group of products. Before a real run, each placeholder needs an exact version and service route. Where that information is unavailable, the result should retain the uncertainty instead of supplying a familiar product name as if it fully specified the candidate.</p>
<p>A useful final report can therefore state both the distinct task count and the attempt count, with the candidate versions and rules attached. The token-cost column then explains what was priced, while the outcome records explain what was learned. Keeping those records together stops a larger run from being mistaken for a broader one.</p>
<p>The unused part of a trial budget is not a reason to add repetitions automatically. Additional cases or a review of ambiguous labels may address the actual uncertainty more directly. Those are editorial planning options here, not measured improvements. The next spending decision should name the question still unanswered and show why more calls would help answer it.</p>
</InsightArticle>
